\section{Define the Boundary of a Minor Collection}

Many programs create short-lived objects while retaining
a smaller stable core. A generational collector exploits
this tendency by collecting a young region more frequently
than an old region. It is a workload hypothesis, not a rule
of the language. Moon's account of ephemeral objects in the
Symbolics 3600 discusses both lifetime classification and
tracking locations containing pointers into the area of
interest. Appel describes software techniques for maintaining
such information \cite{appel2004modern}. The collector does
not need to prove every old object live at each minor
collection. It does need every incoming strong edge from the
uncollected heap into the collected region.

Consider an old queue \(Q\) storing the only reference to a
new request \(R\), which holds new payload \(P\). The root
points to \(Q\). A minor collector that does not walk old
objects will find neither \(R\) nor \(P\) unless it treats
the slot in \(Q\) as an additional root. Record that slot,
the holder object, or a small address range containing it.
The resulting remembered information must cover all actual
old-to-young edges, though it may cover locations that have
since been overwritten and are no longer edges.

\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{lll}
\hline
Remembered unit & Work at store & Work at minor pause \\
\hline
Exact slot & Record slot address & Inspect that slot \\
Holder object & Record holder & Scan its reference fields \\
Card of bytes & Mark small range & Locate and inspect slots in range \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

\Cref{fig:visual-415} separates the edge across the
collection boundary from the edge that will be discovered
by ordinary young-object traversal. The root to \(Q\) can
be ignored by the forwarding routine because its value is
not in the nursery. Ignoring both that value and \(Q\)'s
recorded slot would lose an entire reachable subgraph.

